ys chocolate  cake good some reason market going up  good everyone's else making money got to  get in mark is going down bad i'm losing  my nest egg i want to panic and cell  number four and this is really really  simple but it's far from easy and that  is don't act silly rebalancing works not  every year but in the long run it's the  only true contrarian strategy there are  a lot of contrarian strategies anyone  remember dogs of the Dow that was a  really popular strategy that if you  bought the highest paying dividend  stocks in the Dow the ones that were the  dogs you would have made a ton of money  just so much more than the market as a  whole it had a long track record of  working  once it got publicized guess what it  didn't work anymore my dad who I love  dearly and bought more copies of this  book than my voice even you know called  me and told me I'm a contrarian I'm  following the dogs of the Dow when  you're doing something that everyone is  doing are you a contrarian are you  following the fad it's easy to call  ourselves contrarians but the only true  way to be a contrarian is to rebalance  when stocks go down you have to buy some  more when stocks go up guess what you  have to sell it goes against every human  instinct  now my wife Patti who's in the audience  Kevin and I used to play board games and  we used to have different methods to see  who went first I was one of those guys  we've got to teach him a life lesson he  can't always go first I usually lost  that argument but now Kevin knew that no  matter what method we use think of a  spinner in the book he had a one-third  chance of going first I taught him some  simple math what are the odds of going  first twice in a row one-third times  one-third he could multiply 3 times 3 19  what are your odds of going first three  times in a row 1 out of 27 so a lot of  people come to me and they say why  should I go with an index fund you know  roughly a third of mutual funds beat an  index fund in any given year well I  argued far more than a third I did my  own research on this for jack bogles  book The Little Book of Common Sense  investing and I actually found that  forty-two percent of an active mutual  fund or portfolio with separately  managed to count with the two percent  expense had a forty-two percent chance  of winning but guess what do you know  anyone who just owns one mutual fund or  one portfolio for one year it's kind of  like going to Las Vegas which I love by  the way when I sit down at a table my  best chance of walking out ahead is to  do what play one hand and walk away the  longer I sit at the table and the more  hands i play simultaneously and no i'm  not a card counter the worst my odds get  so here if you have ten mutual funds a  couple us a couple international some  bonds real estate over a one-year period  you've got a twenty-five percent chance  of winning ten funds over a 25-year  period has less than a one percent  chance of winning so put it another way  people play a game with their nest egg  that they have a ninety-nine percent  chance of losing chapter six as Paula  mentioned if there's a better way am I  try it against my wife's better judgment  I tried to teach Kevin correlations  correlations meaning that if something  goes up something else may go down and I  explained to him that there are things  like precious metals and mining stocks  and real estate investment trusts  otherwise known as reads that had not  negative correlations but low  correlations with the US stock market so  that if the stock market did poorly  perhaps those funds could do well and in  the blue by the way is the first bear  market that when the first time the US  stock market lost fifty percent between  two thousand and two thousand and two  wreaths and precious metals did quite  well this bear market different story of  course what caused this bear market real  estate bubble so I didn't get Kevin  there and guess what so far he's been  right  but I'm going to be stubborn and stick  to my ways don't lend money to someone  who won't pay you back never ever forget  the purpose of the bond portfolio it is  to act as a shock absorber so many  people chased a little extra yield an  extra 50 basis points or point five  percent and ended up losing five ten  twenty have a client who came to me with  a munich california muni bond fund that  used all sorts of exotic things and this  is the safe portion of her portfolio  money that she inherited lost about  forty percent worse than the stock  market so it turns out that people with  bad credit can't afford to buy a home  who knew actually everyone knew that but  they thought a real estate can never go  down in value  with that said I do believe that we can  get a little extra yield without taking  risk when you think of it if I'm an  institution with billions of dollars to  invest and I want some risk free money  for a year let's say what do I have to  invest it in a Treasury bill bill that's  yielding about 0.5% but you and I we can  find the highest paying CDs that are  backed by the US government for up to  two hundred and fifty thousand dollars  temporarily through this year for a  taxable account and two hundred and  fifty thousand dollars permanently for  an IRA account that can be paying three  percent now three percent may not sound  like much but I'm going to urge you guys  to get real there are some people in the  room do you remember back in nineteen  eighty-one what CDs were paying about  what the 12 years I've some were as high  as fifteen percent so if the third went  to taxes you've got ten percent left  what was inflation then about thirteen  percent so you're real spending power  dropped by about three percent today if  you can get three percent on a CD you  can actually do better you get two  percent after taxes inflation is at zero  we get a positive two percent return  these are the good old days one of my  columns coming up is about CDs hitting  all-time high rates and in real terms  what matters that's exactly what's going  on  don't bet your lunch money money that  you need in the next five years has  always been too risky to put in the  market again we had five nice steady  years between 2002 and 2007 where we got  gains and each and every year and guess  what we became used to it the market has  always been risky don't pay taxes if you  don't have to this is more of a  technical discussion in the book but  where we locate assets is incredibly  important once you pick an asset  allocation let's say you want to be  sixty percent stocks forty percent bonds  where you place those assets is  incredibly important things that have  tax efficiency already like an index  stock fund belong in your taxable  account things that are taxed at the  highest rates belong in your  tax-deferred accounts that's low hanging  fruit whether the market goes up down or  sideways your return is going to be  higher  if the game is too hard to understand  I'm not going to play keep it simple the  way most people invest I say is a mental  model of a boat designed like this it's  round so if I hire more rowers we're  stronger rowers or Olympic rowers guess  what's going to happen we're going to go  in circles a lot faster paying one  expert more money to outsmart another  expert is not going to work someone else  is going to pay them more money versus  the simple portfolio that just keeps  working has nothing to do with the  efficient market hypothesis has  everything to do with simple  second-grader arithmetic must beat Wall  Street whether the market goes up  whether the market goes down every year  every month every day don't forget the  low-hanging fruit Albert Einstein said  that compounding was the most powerful  force in the universe and I'd like to  nominate inertia as the second most  powerful we just leave our counts  earning lower amounts or maybe we should  pay off the mortgage or maybe we should  do to those things but we never get  around to them pick the low-hanging  fruit and then 13  I have a lot of people come to me and  say your approach is just too simple I'm  more sophisticated well a couple of  people here but that simplicity is  fairly smart and they were a couple of  you know relatively smart people sir  isaac newton in Albert Einstein both  coincidentally lost a fortune in the  stock market  Albert Einstein took the money he won  from the nail belpre Nobel Prize and  invested in what today would be known as  junk bonds he was ahead of his time and  Sir Isaac Newton the South Seas scandal  which was something one of those bubbles  like the tulip bubbles that he lost a  fortune on so the secret to investing is  simplicity and the book goes through the  basic second-grader fund an advanced  second grader portfolio and then an  alternative which takes active and by  the way i call the CD strategy and  active strategy i have abandoned in  large part indexing when it comes to  bonds by trying to find the  highest-paying cds and then where we  locate the accounts again is incredibly  important on a tax-deferred versus a  taxable account  now this is a lot of data here but  basically the 2nd grader portfolio I'm  arguing is right for anyone an  aggressive one might be sixty percent in  US stocks thirty percent international  and ten percent in bonds that's probably  appropriate for no one in this room  maybe a second grader a moderate second  grader portfolio would be sixty percent  in the market and a conservative twenty  percent in the market and again the low  a thirty percent in the market and again  where you locate those funds is  incredibly important it it's not just  your willingness to take risk it's your  need to take risk I see a lot of people  whose objective appears to be dying the  richest person in the graveyard that's  not our goal so in the conclusion one  percent seems like a really really low  number but let me tell you every one  percent you get in additional earning