e lessons that we've all learned by  second grade it will serve us well when  it comes to investing and my friend  Katie Carol gave me a great quote but I  love simplicity is complexity resolved  so my advice to you guys Kevin's and  fifth grade now it's too late find an  eight-year-old describe your whole  investment strategy to them and if they  don't understand it be scared thank you  very much I've really enjoyed this  presentation and we are now going to  take questions and I'm sorry to make  this so formal but if you could either  come up to the mic and ask a question or  they can bring a boom mic to you so  please ask a question sorry to make it  so formal it's a lot last time I give  this presentation there were an hour and  a half worth of questions but we didn't  have to do it this way yes Alan if you  put the safe part of your portfolio in a  total bond index fund don't you expose a  great portion of that to the foibles of  the bond market as in the the housing  bubble and subprime mortgages and take a  beating there if you're in the broad  market total bond fund great question  the total bond market fund is roughly  seventy four percent US government  backed securities so therefore it has a  zero default risk because the US  government is the one entity that's  license to print dollars can do it  legally  it's just a rumor I was not printing  dollars it was just a vicious rumor by a  competitor the other twenty-six percent  are in investment grade bonds now there  should be a little worried there because  guess what wachovia Lehman Brothers Bear  Stearns were all investment grade about  a year and a half ago so there is a  little bit of risk there are also some  other great alternatives I talked about  in the book such as Treasury  inflation-protected securities known as  tips Ginnie Mae is a great bond fund if  you can keep costs low on even of the M  stands for mortgage it is backed by the  US government and those are all bonds  that did either very very well or the  tips didn't do quite as well last year  but you're exposing very little and  again I do think that if you're willing  to do the work finding the  highest-paying CDs and it's an active  strategy because if you find a  high-paying one-year CD let's say that's  paying three and a half percent and you  don't watch your mail guess what the  bank or credit union will do a year  later they've rolled it over to whatever  rate they wanted to give you and you're  locked in at one point two percent for  another year so you do have to actively  manage that thank you  when you talk about rebalancing your  portfolio how often do you do that and  if you do it quite often doesn't that  increase your expense ratio how often  it's not a matter of time it's a matter  of how far it varies from your target so  let's say if you were going to stick to  sixty percent stocks if you got up to  let's say 65 or down to 55 that's the  time to rebalance now that's the  mathematical answer but as I've  hopefully conveyed that even a nerd like  me I'm not a mathematical animal I have  emotions and I will admit when the  market was plummeting I was rebalancing  over a slower pace now I would argue  there's not much of a cost in  rebalancing if you do it with no-load  mutual funds because there's a zero cost  to sell or buy there are ways to do it  more tax efficiently like doing it  within your tax-deferred account and I'm  also a believer and talk about in the  book it's a little more complex and I  could say here and what I call tax loss  harvesting that there are times that you  want to sell just to harvest the tax  loss to be able to use in the future but  there are ways to do it with very very  low costs  what about all these programs that you  hear about on the radio or TV the safety  net ones that guarantee you seven or  eight percent return with absolutely no  risk on the money that you've invested  wouldn't that make more sense absolutely  would make more sense if I could h